# Line endings.
#
# This clone has core.autocrlf=true, which is the right default for a Windows
# checkout: text files are stored LF in the repo and converted to CRLF on the
# way out. One file is not that shape, and it caused a merge conflict nobody
# had earned (2026-10-07).
#
# data/ichgcp_leads.csv is written by scraper/scrape_ichgcp.py through Python's
# csv.writer, whose default line terminator is CRLF (RFC 4180). So the blob in
# the repo genuinely contains CRLF. With autocrlf on, git then cleans the
# working-tree CRLF back to LF to compare it against a blob that has CRLF --
# and reports the file modified when nobody has touched it. That is the source
# of the endless "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it"
# warnings, and when the GitHub Action commits a new version of the same file,
# the phantom local change has a real remote change to conflict with.
#
# `-text` means: no end-of-line conversion either way. Store the bytes the
# scraper wrote, hand back the bytes that are stored, and let the file compare
# equal to itself.
data/*.csv -text

# The same reasoning, pre-emptively, for everything else whose bytes matter as
# bytes rather than as text: a diff of these is not useful and a normalised
# copy is not equivalent.
*.docx binary
*.pyc binary
